  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 324 PM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nThe upper ridge will remain over the Central to Southern Plains \nthrough mid day Tuesday, bringing southwesterly flow across the \nRockies and heading into the the Northern Plains. A deepening low \nwill form across the Pacific Northwest and move eastward across\nsouthern Canada during this time frame as well. Moving down to \nthe surface, a cold front will drop south late Monday and stall \nout Tuesday over central Nebraska. This feature will be the \nprimary focus for the potential of increased shower and \nthunderstorm chances. While these precipitation chances will be\nincreased with a line of 50 to 70 percent chances stretching \nfrom portions of southwest to north central Nebraska, \naccumulations remain fairly lackluster. Guidance suggests a 30 \nto 50 percent chance of areas within that line of higher PoPs \nreceiving at least 0.10\" of wetting rains, with that \nprobability dropping to 10 to 30 percent of receiving 0.25\". \nAdditionally, severe probabilities remain uncertain at this \ntime. Monday look to be the hottest for the week while the upper\nridge remains at its peak, with highs climbing to the mid 90s \nacross the region. Areas along and south of I80 could see highs \nfurther climbing into the upper 90s. Tuesday width aid from the \nincoming cold front will keep highs primarily in the mid to \nupper 80s cross the region. \n\nBy the middle of the week next week, the upper ridge will build \nwestward, creating largely zonal flow. While the aforementioned cold \nfront will push further south out of Nebraska, zonal flow remains \nabove our region and as such high pressure will settle int much of \nthe Plains for the remainder of the week. This will not only keep \nabnormally warm temperatures in the region, where highs will be in \nthe mid to upper 80s (5 to 7 degrees above normal) through Friday; \nbut will largely zap the region for any measureable precipitation \nchances until the upcoming weekend.",
